    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "The average Mongol garrison family of the Yuan dynasty seems to have lived a life of decaying rural leisure, with income from the harvests of their Chinese tenants eaten up by costs of equipping and dispatching men for their tours of duty.",
      "start": 29,
      "end": 268,
      "similarity": -0.006941678619575988,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "The Mongols practiced debt slavery, and by 1290 in all parts of the Mongol Empire commoners were selling their children into slavery.",
      "start": 269,
      "end": 402,
      "similarity": 0.057608228630697624,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "Seeing this as damaging to the Mongol nation, Kublai in 1291 forbade the sale abroad of Mongols.",
      "start": 403,
      "end": 499,
      "similarity": 0.08608315269588143,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "Kublai wished to persuade the Chinese that he was becoming increasingly sinicized while maintaining his Mongolian credentials with his own people.",
      "start": 500,
      "end": 646,
      "similarity": -0.057692412445793595,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "He set up a civilian administration to rule, built a capital within China, supported Chinese religions and culture, and devised suitable economic and political institutions for the court.",
      "start": 647,
      "end": 834,
      "similarity": 0.033697971934285795,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "But at the same time he never abandoned his Mongolian heritage.",
      "start": 835,
      "end": 898,
      "similarity": -0.06514148810302123,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "Since its invention in 1269, the 'Phags-pa script, a unified script for spelling Mongolian, Tibetan, and Chinese languages, was preserved in the court until the end of the dynasty.",
      "start": 913,
      "end": 1093,
      "similarity": 0.028615353135809554,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "Most of the Emperors could not master written Chinese, but they could generally converse well in the language.",
      "start": 1094,
      "end": 1204,
      "similarity": -0.0703668605343236,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "The Mongol custom of long standing quda/marriage alliance with Mongol clans, the Onggirat, and the Ikeres, kept the imperial blood purely Mongol until the reign of Tugh Temur, whose mother was a Tangut concubine.",
      "start": 1205,
      "end": 1417,
      "similarity": -0.033579194801138086,
      "facetSimilarities": []
    },
    {
      "documentId": "76452f5b-8893-449f-b3a4-a38fd657e33b",
      "value": "The Mongol Emperors had built large palaces and pavilions, but some still continued to live as nomads at times.",
